WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.780
There is a file on the internet right now that says what every hospital in Sacramento gets paid.

2
00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:07.500
By each health plan.

3
00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:09.270
For each procedure.

4
00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:11.560
To the cent.

5
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.360
It is public.

6
00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:13.280
It is legal to read.

7
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.900
Almost no human being has ever read it.

8
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:16.520
Nobody hid it.

9
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.150
It is the size.

10
00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:29.110
Federal law said publish the negotiated rates, and they were, in machine files so big that opening one on a laptop is like drinking a reservoir through a straw.

11
00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:31.810
The intent was daylight.

12
00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:37.530
What arrived was a room with the lights on and nobody able to get in the door.

13
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.290
We got through the door.

14
00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:40.220
Every state.

15
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.460
More than five hundred payers.

16
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.330
Nine point two million providers.

17
00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:57.970
Five hundred and eight million rate rows serving live, and behind them a drug rate lake of one point nine three billion rows that answers a question in one and seven tenths of a second.

18
00:00:58.070 --> 00:00:59.880
That is the sound of the file opening.

19
00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:02.310
Here is what is inside.

20
00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:05.800
One common procedure, in Sacramento.

21
00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:12.060
In a doctor's office, the negotiated rate is four hundred seventeen dollars and sixty five cents.

22
00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:16.800
In a surgery center, six hundred seventy nine dollars and ninety two cents.

23
00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:22.710
In a hospital outpatient department, one thousand one hundred nineteen dollars and fifty three cents.

24
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.380
Same procedure, same city.

25
00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:35.760
Four hundred thirty nine dollars and sixty one cents between the surgery center and the hospital, decided by which building the patient sits down in.

26
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.340
A new patient office visit, same city.

27
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.490
Anthem files it at one hundred nine dollars.

28
00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:44.990
Blue Shield at four hundred three.

29
00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:46.500
Both published.

30
00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.420
Neither on anybody's screen at renewal time.

31
00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.040
And rates are only the filed half of the story.

32
00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:56.950
Pharmacy is a quarter of plan spend and the fastest growing line on it, and we hold all of it.

33
00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.160
What the drug costs to acquire.

34
00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.410
What the pharmacy benefit manager was paid.

35
00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.050
The rebate, and who kept it.

36
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:10.720
The spread between the price on paper and the price in practice, line by line, drug by drug.

37
00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:15.180
We carry the volume, how often a code is actually filled in a market.

38
00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:26.820
And we carry what was actually paid on the claim, next to what was filed on the contract, so the deal on paper and the deal in practice sit in two columns on one screen.

39
00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.540
Now think about the meeting you had last spring.

40
00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:32.810
The employer leaned back and asked the honest question.

41
00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:34.540
Are we paying too much.

42
00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:39.150
And the honest answer available to anyone in that chair was a feeling.

43
00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:41.420
The market is tough this year.

44
00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:42.400
Everyone is up.

45
00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.000
That is what the closed file costs.

46
00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:46.000
Not a line item.

47
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:49.900
A whole category of conversation you could not have.

48
00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.910
Sacramento has the highest share of scoreable cells of any metro in California.

49
00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:59.100
Fifty one point four percent of peer cells are scoreable.

50
00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:01.010
Grade A.

51
00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.820
Your market is where this works best.

52
00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:06.870
You have sat through the other demos.

53
00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:13.030
Someone slid a confident figure across the table, you asked where it came from, and the room got vague.

54
00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:14.450
That is the problem.

55
00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:15.720
Not insurers.

56
00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:17.170
Opacity.

57
00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.100
A health plan pays for the room you are standing in.

58
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.820
The enemy is that nobody will tell you how the number was made.

59
00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:25.330
So we tell you.

60
00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:28.760
And when we do not know, the screen says so.

61
00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:41.380
Ask for the standard office visit, code 99213, in Sacramento, and you get a refusal printed in plain sight: seventeen filings, and our publishing floor is one hundred.

62
00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:52.300
In the federal payment dispute record, San Francisco shows one thousand eight hundred seventy resolved lines at one point nine one times the benchmark.

63
00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:58.580
Sacramento gives you a refusal at four hundred thirty six, under the higher floor that record carries.

64
00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:09.250
Kaiser publishes no negotiated rate file at all, because it is a closed system, so we say that instead of inventing a number to fill the box.

65
00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:15.070
A tool that never says I do not know is lying to you somewhere, and you will not know where.

66
00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:17.220
Now here is the turn.

67
00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:19.550
Rates are what everyone expects.

68
00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:24.570
What changes your Monday is that the same discipline runs across your whole book.

69
00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:27.730
Now here is the one nobody sees coming.

70
00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:40.690
Every employer in America with a hundred people on a health plan has to tell the federal government, every single year, exactly who insures them, who their broker is, and what that broker got paid.

71
00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:42.190
In writing.

72
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.990
On a form called the fifty five hundred.

73
00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:47.880
And then the government does the funniest thing.

74
00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:49.430
It publishes all of it.

75
00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:51.720
{air:0.

76
00:04:51.720 --> 00:05:01.620
9} A hundred and fifty thousand filings a year, dropped into zip files so ugly that in twenty years, nobody in this industry ever bothered to open them.

77
00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:03.900
We opened every one.

78
00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:05.130
Every state.

79
00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:06.920
Every year on record.

80
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:08.570
So picture this.

81
00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:11.070
You are a general agency.

82
00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:13.660
You point one of your producers at a market.

83
00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:15.210
Any market.

84
00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:16.940
{air:0.

85
00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:22.690
8} And the register hands them every employer in it, sorted by the month their plan renews.

86
00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:24.690
The carrier they are with.

87
00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.010
The broker who has them today.

88
00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:29.780
What that broker was paid last year, to the dollar.

89
00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:31.440
{air:1.

90
00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.960
0} How many people are on the plan and whether it is insured or self funded.

91
00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:38.340
The month the plan year turns.

92
00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:39.750
{air:0.

93
00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:42.970
9} And the phone number, straight off the filing.

94
00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:49.090
Twenty thousand two hundred twenty one of the twenty thousand three hundred ninety two California filings carry one.

95
00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:53.340
Where the filing names the plan administrator, you get that too.

96
00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:56.010
That is not a lead list.

97
00:05:56.390 --> 00:06:02.950
That is the entire territory, describing itself, in public, for twenty years, while everyone drove past it.

98
00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:05.340
The ninety day window before a renewal?

99
00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:07.680
You see it open before the incumbent does.

100
00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:09.700
{air:1.

101
00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:12.820
0} Which producer gets the call?

102
00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:16.120
The one holding the register.

103
00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:17.810
People ask how we pulled this off.

104
00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:20.550
Honestly, the files were sitting right there.

105
00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:31.500
What did not exist was the machine to read a hundred fifty thousand of them, join every employer to the rate record, and put a live renewal clock on every row.

106
00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:33.530
{air:0.

107
00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:35.770
9} We built that machine.

108
00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:40.260
In California alone: eight thousand four hundred sixty seven employers.

109
00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:45.380
Three thousand two hundred seventy in the Bay Area and Sacramento corridors.

110
00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:49.650
Three hundred forty eight in Sacramento itself.

111
00:06:50.150 --> 00:07:00.710
Every group you serve sits in that same operator register, with the renewal clock counting down, so the ninety day window never arrives as a surprise in a carrier email.

112
00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.730
Beside each group, a dossier built from the record.

113
00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:12.450
Three thousand two hundred seventy in the Bay Area and Sacramento corridors, three hundred forty eight in Sacramento itself.

114
00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:23.510
Nine hundred sixty eight million dollars of broker commission filed on California Schedule A for plan year 2023, across fifty two thousand two hundred sixty eight rows.

115
00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:29.200
That is your industry, describing itself, in public, all this time.

116
00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:37.680
Three hundred thirty six thousand three hundred twenty California provider affiliation rows, twenty two thousand two hundred twenty named groups.

117
00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:43.580
So who is actually in this network near our warehouse is an answer, not a project.

118
00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:45.060
Then the documents.

119
00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:46.650
The rate exhibit.

120
00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:47.960
The market memo.

121
00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:49.080
The renewal brief.

122
00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:51.190
You do not hand over a screenshot.

123
00:07:51.890 --> 00:08:01.360
You hand over an exhibit with your firm's name on it, your language, the sources cited underneath, the refusals still printed where refusals belong.

124
00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:05.150
And the chief financial officer stops asking why it is going up.

125
00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:07.130
He starts asking what we do about it.

126
00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:08.900
That is a different meeting.

127
00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:11.530
That is a renewal you keep for five years.

128
00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:14.210
The automations keep watch while you sell.

129
00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:22.430
Thirteen codes retire on the thirtieth of September, inside renewal season, and you hear about it before the plan sponsor does.

130
00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:32.220
Eight thousand seven hundred sixty nine supply and equipment codes carry plain English names, so a wheelchair cushion reads like a wheelchair cushion.

131
00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:36.390
And you can ask your own book a question in plain words.

132
00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:39.170
Which of my groups is exposed to that hospital rate.

133
00:08:39.290 --> 00:08:40.860
The answer comes back sourced.

134
00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:43.190
Your book stays in this browser.

135
00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:47.460
No patient information, no uploads, ever.

136
00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:50.750
One last figure, labelled the way the screen labels it.

137
00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:57.140
A two hundred fifty life California plan spends about three point nine five million dollars a year.

138
00:08:57.950 --> 00:09:08.660
That figure is modelled, from federal survey data, trended forward, with an honest range of fifteen thousand eight hundred to eighteen thousand five hundred dollars per employee.

139
00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:13.130
Mercer measured seventeen thousand four hundred ninety six in 2025.

140
00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:17.980
Modelled, not measured, and we say so in front of your client.

141
00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:24.390
In a market that sells confident numbers, the one willing to tell you no is the only one worth believing.

142
00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:25.550
Book a discovery call.

143
00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:27.350
Twenty minutes.

144
00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:31.640
Your metro, your codes, your groups, on the screen, with the refusals showing.

145
00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:34.824
Then walk into your next renewal holding the file.
